

00:01:11.953 --> 00:01:16.712
For my project on invertebrates, I chose to study tardigrades.

00:01:16.712 --> 00:01:21.979
These are microscopic or to be more precise near microscopic animals.

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:29.549
There are well over a thousand known species of these tiny animals which belong to the phylum Tardigrada.

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:41.738
Most tardigrades range in length from 0.05 to 1mm, though the largest species can grow to be 1.2 mm in length.

00:01:41.738 --> 00:01:50.712
They're also sometimes called water bears, water because that's where they thrive best, and bear because of the way they move.

00:01:50.712 --> 00:01:57.953
Moss piglet is another name for tardigrades because of the way they look when viewed from the front.

00:01:57.953 --> 00:02:07.644
They were first discovered in Germany, in 1773, by Johann Goetzer, who coined the name Tardigrade.

00:02:07.644 --> 00:02:12.162
As I say, there are many different species of tardigrade.

00:02:12.162 --> 00:02:19.504
Too many to describe here, but generally speaking, the different species share similar physical traits.

00:02:19.504 --> 00:02:25.564
They have a body which is short, and also rounded, a bit like a barrel.

00:02:25.564 --> 00:02:28.835
And the body comprises four segments.

00:02:28.835 --> 00:02:36.205
Each segment has a pair of legs, at the end of which are between four and eight sharp claws.

00:02:36.205 --> 00:02:40.653
I should also say that some species don't have any claws.

00:02:40.653 --> 00:02:42.998
What they have are discs.

00:02:42.998 --> 00:02:46.191
And these work by means of suction.

00:02:46.191 --> 00:02:51.991
They enable the tardigrade to cling on to surfaces, or grip its prey.

00:02:51.991 --> 00:02:57.086
Within the body, there are no lungs or any organs for breathing at all.

00:02:57.086 --> 00:03:05.510
Instead, oxygen and also blood are transported in a fluid that fills the cavity of the body.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:22.691
As far as the tardigrade's head is concerned, the best way I can describe this is that it looks rather strange, a bit squashed even, though many of the websites I looked at described its appearance as cute, which isn't exactly very scientific.

00:03:22.691 --> 00:03:31.466
The tardigrade's mouth is a kind of tube that can open outwards to reveal teeth-like structures known as stylates.

00:03:31.466 --> 00:03:36.297
These are sharp enough to pierce plant or animal cells.

00:03:36.297 --> 00:03:39.263
So where are tardigrades found?

00:03:39.263 --> 00:03:42.582
Well, they live in every part of the world.

00:03:42.582 --> 00:03:52.694
In a variety of habitats, most commonly on the bed of a lake, or on many kinds of plants or in very wet environments.

00:03:52.694 --> 00:04:01.483
There's been some interesting research which has found that tardigrades are capable of surviving radiation and very high pressure.

00:04:01.483 --> 00:04:15.850
And they're also able to withstand temperatures as cold as minus 200 degrees centigrade, or highs of more than 148 degrees centigrade, which is incredibly hot.

00:04:20.124 --> 00:04:29.937
It has been said that tardigrades could survive long after human beings have been wiped out, even in the event of an asteroid hitting the Earth.

00:04:29.937 --> 00:04:38.832
If conditions become too extreme and tardigrades are at risk of drying out, they enter a state called cryptobiosis.

00:04:38.832 --> 00:04:51.522
They curl into a ball called a tun, that's T-U-N, by retracting their head and legs, and their metabolism drops to less than 1% of normal levels.

00:04:51.522 --> 00:04:59.241
They can remain like this until they're reintroduced to water when they will come back to life in a matter of a few hours.

00:04:59.241 --> 00:05:07.221
While in a state of cryptobiosis, tardigrades produce a protein that protects their DNA.

00:05:07.221 --> 00:05:14.947
In 2016, scientists revived two tardigrades that had been tons for more than 30 years.

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:22.179
There was a report that in 1948, a 120-year-old ton was revived.

00:05:22.179 --> 00:05:25.288
But this experiment has never been repeated.

00:05:25.288 --> 00:05:34.563
There are currently several tests taking place in space to determine how long tardigrades might be able to survive there.

00:05:34.563 --> 00:05:38.135
I believe the record so far is 10 days.

00:05:38.135 --> 00:05:40.553
So, moving on.

00:05:40.553 --> 00:05:47.166
In terms of their diet, tardigrades consume liquids in order to survive.

00:05:47.166 --> 00:05:51.309
Although they have teeth, they don't use these for chewing.

00:05:51.309 --> 00:05:56.978
They suck the juices from moss or extract fluid from seaweed.

00:05:56.978 --> 00:06:03.476
But some species prey on other tardigrades, from other species or within their own.

00:06:03.476 --> 00:06:12.285
I suppose this isn't surprising given that tardigrades are mainly comprised of liquid and are coated with a type of gel.

00:06:12.285 --> 00:06:17.300
Finally, I'd like to mention the conservation status of tardigrades.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:28.180
It is estimated that they have been in existence for approximately half a billion years and, in that time, they have survived five mass extinctions.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:41.306
So it will probably come as no surprise to you that tardigrades have not been evaluated by the International Union for Conservation of Nature and are not on any endangered list.

00:06:41.306 --> 00:06:45.972
Some researchers have described them as thriving.

00:06:45.972 --> 00:06:49.912
Does anyone have any questions they'd like to ask?
